## What the law said on 1 October 2025

The applicable document is the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version No. 110**, incorporating amendments as at **6 August 2025** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

- The property had to be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- The rental provider or agent could not **solicit or invite** anyone to offer more than the advertised rent. Breach carried a maximum penalty of **60 penalty units for a natural person or 300 penalty units for a body corporate** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- Advertising could not make a **false or misleading representation about the rent** [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- However, on 1 October 2025, the Act did **not yet prohibit accepting an unsolicited higher offer**. That prohibition appears only in the later **Authorised Version No. 111 dated 25 November 2025**, as new section 30F(4), so it does not govern your situation [[19]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf).
- The Act does not say that applying at the advertised price guarantees you the property, and the retrieved documents do not expressly prohibit withdrawing one advertisement and later advertising at a different fixed rent [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- The false email saying that **you withdrew** is concerning, but the retrieved Act does not expressly create a remedy for that statement alone. It may support an argument that the original rent advertising or application process was misleading, depending on the surrounding evidence [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

## What you can do or ask

You have a basis to challenge or report the conduct **if there is evidence that the provider or agent invited higher bids**, or that the original advertised rent was false or misleading [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf). Merely hearing that another applicant voluntarily offered more would not establish a breach of the law then in force [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

Ask the agent in writing to:

1. correct the record that you did not withdraw;
2. confirm who marked your application as withdrawn and when;
3. explain why the property was relisted at a higher rent; and
4. confirm whether any applicant was asked, encouraged or invited to offer above the advertised amount.

The retrieved documents do not clearly identify a specific VCAT application or remedy for an unsuccessful applicant alleging a breach of sections 30F or 30G, so I cannot say from these documents alone that VCAT could order the property to be rented to you.

## Evidence to save

Keep original, dated copies of:

- the first advertisement showing the property, advertised rent and date;
- your completed application and submission confirmation;
- all emails, texts and portal notifications, especially the false withdrawal email;
- your written response stating that you did not withdraw;
- the later advertisement showing the higher rent and its publication date;
- any message or application field inviting applicants to nominate a higher rent;
- notes of phone calls, including date, time, person and exact words used;
- any direct statement from the other applicant about what they offered and whether the agent invited it; and
- screenshots with the full URL, date and time visible where possible.

These materials address the key legal questions: what fixed amount was advertised, whether higher offers were solicited or invited, and whether the rent advertising was false or misleading [[17]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

## What the law said on 1 October 2025

The applicable law was the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version No. 110**, incorporating amendments as at **6 August 2025** and in force from 6 August to 24 November 2025. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

- A property had to be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent**. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The rental provider or agent could not **solicit or invite** an offer above the advertised rent. Breach carried a maximum penalty of **60 penalty units for a natural person or 300 penalty units for a corporation**. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- Advertising could not make a **false or misleading representation about the rent**, with the same maximum penalties. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- However, this version did **not expressly prohibit an applicant from making an unsolicited higher offer, or the provider from accepting one**. The critical issue is whether the provider or agent encouraged or invited that higher offer. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- Removing the original listing and later advertising a genuinely new fixed price is not expressly prohibited by the retrieved Act. But the sequence may support a concern that the original rent advertising was false or misleading. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The retrieved Act does not specifically address an agent falsely recording that an applicant withdrew. It therefore does not establish, by itself, that the incorrect withdrawal email was a separate tenancy-law breach. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What you can do or ask

You can report the circumstances to the **Director of Consumer Affairs Victoria**, whose functions include monitoring compliance with the Act. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf) Ask the agent, in writing, to:

1. correct the record that you did **not** withdraw;
2. explain when, why and by whom your application was marked withdrawn;
3. confirm whether anyone was invited or encouraged to offer above the original advertised rent; and
4. preserve the application and advertising records.

The retrieved Act does **not clearly give an unsuccessful prospective renter a direct VCAT application specifically for a breach of sections 30F or 30G**. Its general VCAT provision primarily covers providers and renters under a residential rental agreement; a non-party may apply only with VCAT’s leave in relation to an agreement and must show sufficient interest and personal involvement. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf) The documents therefore do not establish that you can obtain the property or compensation through VCAT on these facts.

## Evidence to save

Keep material showing each part of the sequence:

- screenshots or archived copies of the **original advertisement**, including the address, fixed rent, date and agent;
- your completed application and submission confirmation showing the rent offered;
- the email saying you withdrew, including full headers and timestamp;
- your written reply stating that you did not withdraw;
- screenshots of the later advertisement showing the higher rent and publication date;
- every email, text, portal message or note of a call suggesting that you—or anyone else—should offer more;
- the name and exact account of anyone who says a higher offer was invited, distinguishing what they personally saw from hearsay; and
- a dated timeline of the application, withdrawal message, relisting and all conversations.

These records are relevant because the legal questions are whether rent was offered as a fixed amount, whether a higher offer was solicited or invited, and whether the rent advertising was false or misleading. [[7]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
